{{also|Alderman}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{etyl|ang|en}} {{m|ang|aldormann}} or {{m|ang|ealdormann}}; equivalent to {{m|en|elder}} + {{m|en|man}}. See {{m|en|ealdorman}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{hyphenation|al|der|man|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|aldermen}}

# A member of a [[municipal]] [[legislative]] [[body]] in a [[city]] or [[town]].
# {{lb|en|UK|historical|obsolete|_|slang}} A [[half-crown]] [[coin]]; its [[value]], 30 [[old pence|pence]].
#* '''1859''', {{w|J.C. Hotten}}, ''{{w|A Dictionary of Modern Slang, Cant, and Vulgar Words}}''
#*: ''Half-a-crown'' is known as an {{smallcaps|[[alderman]]}}, {{smallcaps|half a [[bull]]}}, {{smallcaps|half a [[tusheroon]]}}, and a {{smallcaps|[[madza caroon]]}}; [[whilst]] a ''crown'' piece, or ''five [[shilling]]s'', may be called either a {{smallcaps|[[bull]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[caroon]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[cartwheel]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[coachwheel]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[thick-un]]}}, or a {{smallcaps|[[tusheroon]]}}.
# {{lb|en|obsolete|slang}}<!--{{context|obsolete|slang|lang=en}}--> A roasted turkey. {{only used in|alderman in chains|lang=en}} and '''{{l|en|alderman hung in chains}}''' {{gloss|"roasted turkey garnished with sausages"}}.

